# Build context for zk/operator/Dockerfile.
#
# The awkward part is `target/`: it is enormous and must not be sent, EXCEPT for the guest ELFs,
# which live under `zk/*/target/elf-compilation/` and are the one build output the image genuinely
# needs (they are compiled outside the image — see the Dockerfile header for why). Docker applies
# every pattern in order and the last match wins, so the negations below re-include exactly those
# paths out of the blanket exclusion above them.

**/target
**/node_modules
**/.git
**/.next
**/.turbo

!zk/program/target/elf-compilation/**
!zk/trustgraph-program-v2/target/elf-compilation/**
!zk/weighted-program/target/elf-compilation/**
!zk/composition-program/target/elf-compilation/**
!zk/nostr-program/program/target/elf-compilation/**

# ...but not the intermediate build state inside them. Only the ELFs.
zk/**/elf-compilation/**/deps/**
zk/**/elf-compilation/**/build/**
zk/**/elf-compilation/**/incremental/**
zk/**/elf-compilation/**/examples/**
zk/**/elf-compilation/**/.fingerprint/**

# Local state and generated artifacts. None of it belongs in an image, and `.demo` and
# `.trustgraph` in particular can hold a request journal and a submitter's working files.
.demo
.trustgraph
.docker
broadcast
cache
out
deployments/**/dev*
repos
docs
research
packages/frontend/.next
**/*.log
